/**
 * Programs on a CUDA device. Where a computation runs is asked for in the source with the tag
 * `#gpu`, written on a function, a block or a namespace block (design principles 13.4), and the
 * card is chosen when the program runs, with `--devices`. The pass `tiles::compile` reads
 * the tags, outlines the regions the device can run, compiles them and runs the program: an
 * operator the device cannot run inside a `#gpu` scope is a compile error that names the operator
 * and the scope, never a quiet fall back to the host. An array on the device is an ordinary array
 * value whose data block lives there; the host reading it (printing, writing a file, turning it
 * into a host number) downloads it, and the reports of a run list every such transfer.
 *
 * What a program does by hand is small: ask whether a device is there (`available`, `device`), move
 * a value (`upload`, `download`), and look at what would run (`source`, `inspect`, `benchmark`,
 * `regions`). The device and the driver are one resource, `driver`, so every call here keeps its
 * written order against the others and is never folded, merged or dropped. The functions to look
 * at are the `with` argument and the data is the example input, as for `map`:
 * `inputs->inspect<fn>`.
 *
 * The functions that compute their type from an argument (`upload`, `download`, `source`,
 * `inspect`, `benchmark`, `regions`, and `run_preferred_resident`, the entry `tiles::compile` and
 * the tests use) are in `stdlib/signatures/tiles.cml`.
 */

/// A value that lives on a device, with the host type of what it holds: `Device<f32[3]>`.
export type Device<T>

/// The CUDA driver and the devices behind it, as one resource: every use of the device is a use
/// of this.
export resource driver

/// Whether this machine can run a program on a CUDA device: false when the driver, the toolkit or
/// a visible device is missing. A capability question only -- a device the run names with
/// `--devices` and that is not there is a fault at start-up, never `false`.
/// @example available()
export func available(): bool uses driver

/// A description of the CUDA device the run selects: its ordinal, name, compute capability and
/// memory.
/// @fails error value `DeviceUnavailable` when this build or this machine has no CUDA device
/// @example device() !! 'no device'
export func device(): string! uses driver

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Passes: functions the pipeline applies to the program
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Outlines the regions the device can run, compiles them and executes the program; the card is
/// chosen at run time with `--devices`, never by a pass parameter.
/// @fails error value `GpuScopeUnsupported` when a `#gpu` scope holds an operator the device cannot run (the error names the operator and the scope)
export func compile::<F>(f: F): F! uses driver when is_function::<F>()

/// Prints the operator surface of the program and consumes it: one `TILES_CENSUS` line with the
/// number of surface operators, how many the device's capability table names and how many it does
/// not, then one `TILES_CENSUS_DEVICE` line per operator the table names and one `TILES_CENSUS_GAP`
/// line per operator no device program exists for. The surface is what a `#gpu` scope asks the
/// device for (the `tensor` and `nn` modules); a `#gpu` scope refuses every operator of the gap, so
/// the list is the work the support surface has left. It reads the program where the pipeline
/// stands, which is why `std::macro` is written before it when a derived graph's operators are to be
/// counted.
export func census::<F>(f: F): F! when is_function::<F>()
/// The outline pass the tests drive directly, with the readings they assert as parameters rather
/// than as environment variables: `reasons=true` prints, per operator node of the result, the
/// declaration and the reason it is in no region (`TILES_OPERATOR_DECLARATION`,
/// `TILES_OPERATOR_ID`, `TILES_REGION_REASON`), and `declarations=true` prints the six batches of
/// the twenty-one operators of the B group with their ids and contracts (`TILES_DECLARATIONS_1`
/// through `TILES_DECLARATIONS_6`). Both default to false: a pipeline that does not ask prints
/// `TILES_OUTLINED` and nothing else.
/// @fails error value `GpuScopeUnsupported` when the outlined region holds an operator the device cannot run
with <reasons: bool = false, declarations: bool = false>
export func test_outline_compiled::<F>(f: F): F! when is_function::<F>()